; 20/F057 (B156 list B, review): on PULP an integer add reduction is kept in the loop, and
; only the shapes the dot-product combine turns into pv.dot*/pv.sdot* are worth vectorizing.
; A reduction step on 32-bit lanes that is not one of them (a scalar that does not fit the
; lane, signed x unsigned-only scalar, an int32 scalar, a product of non-extended values, a
; shift, a product the reduction subtracts) is the scalar loop plus a pv.extract per lane,
; slower than the scalar p.lb/p.lh + p.mac loop (up to 1.32x, sim=legacy): these loops must
; stay scalar. A strided operand of a dot product is loaded lane by lane without
; post-increment addressing (1.36x slower): also scalar. Controls: a mixed-sign dot product
; and a dot product by a scalar that fits still vectorize. Counts of a compare (cnt32,
; cnt16) must not become in-loop reductions of a zext <N x i1>, which InstCombine turns into
; a mask bitcast that ISel cannot lower (the second RUN line aborted in llc): cnt32 stays
; scalar, cnt16 keeps the out-of-loop vector form.

